{
 "id": "2026-11-15-starship-ship-catch",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "A spaceship returned from orbit, and a tower caught it. Why does that matter?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "On Saturday, SpaceX's Starship upper stage flew back to its launch site in South Texas. Two giant tower arms grabbed it in midair. NBC News and CNBC report no stage returning from orbit had been caught before."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "It launched Friday. Minutes later, the giant Super Heavy booster called off its own catch and came down in the Gulf of Mexico. SpaceX says it is investigating. The ship released twenty-six Starlink satellites and circled Earth fourteen times in about twenty-two hours, CNBC reports."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "SpaceX says catching both stages should let it inspect and refly rockets quickly, like an aircraft. Elon Musk says the booster loss does not change his timeline. How much repair a caught ship needs is still unknown."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will a caught Starship fly again before summer twenty twenty-seven? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
